  "Name": "Dobrowolski1970-filterF-ybar",
  "Source": "J. A. Dobrowolski, \"Optical Interference Filters for the Adjustment of Spectral Response and Spectral Power Distribution,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 1396-1402 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.001396 - Table II (Filter F), p. 1401; Sec. III.E, Fig. 12",
  "Notes": "y-bar tristimulus filter (CIE standard observer colour-matching function) for use with a Gillod-Boutry photocell, in series with absorbing glass cutoff filters (Sec. III.E)\nSpecification: Relative transmittance of the interference filter x glass filters (9.98 mm GG-5 and 2.0 mm KG-1, as labelled in Fig. 12(a); glass data not given) x photocell sensitivity (Fig. 9) should match the CIE 1931 y-bar colour-matching function (solid target curve of Fig. 12(a)); only the relative shape was specified, any peak transmittance > 50 % acceptable.\nReported: Peak transmittance of the y-bar filter combination: calculated 87 % (allowing for two air-glass surfaces), experimental 84 %, 76 % with correction filters (text p. 1401-1402, Fig. 12). The computed transmittance of the 17-layer filter alone (dashed, Fig. 12(a)) peaks ~0.94 near 0.555 um, with blocked-by-glass sidebands ~0.72 near 0.40 um and ~0.96 near 0.44 um (plot readings). Fig. 12(b): calculated (target - normalized transmittance) of the cutoff + interference filter combination; worst case among the three filters (x-bar) is < +/-5 % of peak transmittance.\nNotes: Medium and substrate both 1.514 as printed (filter cemented between glass); the 'two air-glass surfaces' in the reported peak transmittances are the outer faces of the assembly. Identification: Fig. 12 is labelled 17-LAYER FILTER, F is the only 17-layer design. Table lists Substrate, layers 1..N, Medium; layer 1 is next to the substrate (the synthesis program grows the design from the bare substrate), so the order is REVERSED here (layer 1 below = last printed layer, next to the medium). Thicknesses are printed as optical thicknesses n*d in micrometres; they are entered as FWOT at reference_nm = 1000 nm, which is the same number (1 FWOT at 1000 nm = 1 um optical thickness), with constant, non-dispersive, lossless indices exactly as printed. Physical thickness d = OT/n. Normal incidence assumed (not stated). 17 layers, as stated in the text; summed optical thickness of the printed layers = 3.0797 um (no total is printed).\nLayers from Table II (Filter F), p. 1401; Sec. III.E, Fig. 12; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours peak T 94.5 % at 570 nm; paper peak ~0.94 near 555 nm (Fig. 12a).",
